# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATED by `vidra setup` from deploy/Caddyfile — do not hand-edit.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-run `vidra setup` to regenerate it; anything written here is lost when that
# happens. Hand edits belong in deploy/Caddyfile, the template this was
# rendered from — everything below except the site address and the TLS settings
# is that file, byte for byte.
#
# The `caddy` service in docker-compose.prod.yml bind-mounts THIS file at
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, so a change takes effect on a `caddy reload`.

# Reference TLS reverse proxy for a single-host Vidra deployment (Caddy v2).
#
# Used by the `caddy` service in docker-compose.prod.yml, which bind-mounts this
# file read-only at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile. Replace `example.com` with your domain
# (and only your domain — Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt certificate for every
# hostname in a site block, so a stray placeholder means a failed ACME order).
# deploy/deploy.sh enforces this: it refuses to deploy while any NON-comment line
# of this file still contains example.com. The comments below may keep it.
#
# ============================================================================
# SINGLE-ORIGIN ROUTING — this is a deliberate architectural decision.
# ============================================================================
# Everything is served from ONE hostname and split by path: the api gets its own
# routes, the Next.js frontend gets everything else.
#
# Why: vidra-core builds absolute URLs from a SINGLE config value,
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL, for two different kinds of thing —
#   * user-facing watch/embed/channel links (internal/httpapi/distribution.go:61,
#     consumed by the RSS feed, /sitemap.xml and the oEmbed iframe src), and
#   * API URLs: OAuth callbacks (oauth.go:136), NodeInfo (federation.go:48) and
#     the ActivityPub/atproto actor URIs (cmd/api/main.go:1103/1118/1168),
# and internal/config/config.go:1009 forbids PUBLIC_BASE_URL from carrying a path
# component. On a split-origin deployment (api.example.com + example.com) there
# is no value that satisfies both: point it at the api host and every feed item
# link, every <loc> in the sitemap and every oEmbed embed 404s; point it at the
# web host and OAuth + federation break instead.
#
# Serving both from example.com makes PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://example.com and
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://example.com simultaneously correct, makes CORS
# same-origin, and needs no Go change. The namespaces do not collide: the
# frontend's /videos/{id} and the api's /api/v1/videos/{id} are distinct, and the
# api's root-mounted federation routes (/accounts, /video-channels) do not exist
# in the frontend (it uses /users and /channels).
#
# A split-subdomain variant is kept at the bottom of this file, with the caveat.
{
	# vidra:global-options
	# ^ MARKER — do not remove or reword. `vidra setup` renders
	# deploy/Caddyfile.local from this file and replaces the marker line with
	# the global options it manages (`email <VIDRA_ACME_EMAIL>`, and the
	# staging CA endpoint when VIDRA_TLS_MODE=acme-staging). Everything else in
	# this file is copied through verbatim, which is why the routing body below
	# can be hand-tuned without fighting the generator.
	#
	# Uncomment and set a real address: Let's Encrypt uses it for expiry and
	# revocation notices. Without it you get no warning before a renewal
	# problem becomes an outage.
	# email ops@example.com

	# Caddy's admin API listens on 127.0.0.1:2019 INSIDE the container and is
	# not published by docker-compose.prod.yml, so it is not reachable from the
	# host or the network. Uncomment to remove it entirely.
	# admin off
}

video.example.org {
	# vidra:tls
	tls internal
	# ^ MARKER — do not remove or reword. `vidra setup` replaces this line in
	# deploy/Caddyfile.local with the site-level TLS directive implied by
	# VIDRA_TLS_MODE: nothing at all for `acme` (Caddy's default is already
	# Let's Encrypt production), and `tls internal` for `internal`, which
	# issues from Caddy's own local CA for a LAN/VPN-only instance. The site
	# ADDRESS above is rewritten from PUBLIC_BASE_URL at the same time —
	# deploy.sh refuses to deploy while the two disagree.

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# 1. Hard 404 for the Prometheus scrape.
	#
	# internal/httpapi/server.go:806 mounts /metrics at the ROOT with NO auth
	# middleware (it is deliberately outside the OpenAPI contract and outside
	# the /api/v1 rate-limited group). It only exists when METRICS_ENABLED=true,
	# but this block means flipping that switch later can never publish request
	# rates, route templates and queue depths to the internet. Scrape it from
	# inside the compose network instead (a `monitoring` profile), never here.
	handle /metrics {
		respond 404
	}

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# 2. Defense in depth for the dev-only mail-capture route.
	#
	# GET /api/v1/dev/email-token returns a live password-reset token for ANY
	# address. The application already gates it twice (config.validate() refuses
	# DEV_MAIL_CAPTURE_ENABLED in production, and server.go:1100 additionally
	# refuses to register the route when Environment == "production"), so this
	# is belt-and-braces — but it costs nothing and it survives a future
	# refactor of either gate.
	handle /api/v1/dev/* {
		respond 404
	}

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# 3. Backend surfaces -> vidra-core.
	#
	# `api:8080` is the COMPOSE SERVICE NAME, not localhost: Caddy runs in its
	# own container, so `localhost` would be Caddy itself. Do not "fix" this to
	# 127.0.0.1.
	#
	# The list below is derived from the actual root-level route registrations
	# in vidra-core/internal/httpapi/server.go:794-848 — everything the api
	# serves OUTSIDE /api/v1. Several are conditional (federation discovery only
	# when FEDERATION_ENABLED, the distribution surfaces only when
	# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is set); routing them unconditionally is harmless, the api
	# simply answers 404 when they are not mounted.
	#
	#   /api/*                  the REST API              (server.go:849)
	#   /healthz /readyz        liveness / readiness      (server.go:794-795)
	#   /version                build metadata            (server.go:796)
	#   /.well-known/nodeinfo   NodeInfo discovery        (server.go:814)
	#   /nodeinfo/2.1           NodeInfo document         (server.go:815)
	#   /.well-known/webfinger  WebFinger                 (server.go:816)
	#   /accounts/*             AP actor + inbox +
	#   /video-channels/*       followers/following/outbox(server.go:817-831)
	#   /inbox                  AP shared inbox           (server.go:821)
	#   /feeds/*                RSS 2.0 (feeds/videos.xml)(server.go:844)
	#   /services/oembed        oEmbed provider           (server.go:845)
	#   /sitemap.xml            XML sitemap               (server.go:846)
	#
	# NOTE on /.well-known: only the two paths the api actually serves are
	# listed, NOT the whole namespace — /.well-known/acme-challenge/* must stay
	# with Caddy for the HTTP-01 certificate challenge.
	@api path /api/* \
 /healthz /readyz /version \
 /.well-known/nodeinfo /.well-known/webfinger /nodeinfo/2.1 \
 /accounts/* /video-channels/* /inbox \
 /feeds/* /services/oembed /sitemap.xml
	handle @api {
		# NO `encode` in this block. The api is the media origin: it serves HLS
		# segments, progressive MP4/M4A and original downloads with
		# `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and 206 Partial Content (see the CDN policy
		# table in ./README.md). Compressing at the edge is worthless on
		# already-compressed media and is the classic way to break Range and
		# seeking. JSON responses here are small; the win is not worth the risk.
		reverse_proxy api:8080 {
			# Caddy already appends X-Forwarded-For / -Proto / -Host. Once
			# vidra-core sets echo's IPExtractor (plan step 5a-iii) that is what
			# makes the per-IP auth limiter see the real client instead of the
			# Caddy container.
			#
			# No response timeouts are set on purpose: resumable 8 MiB chunk
			# PUTs and long media reads must not be cut off at the edge.
			# Server-Sent Events (GET /api/v1/admin/jobs/events) are detected by
			# Caddy from the text/event-stream content type and flushed
			# immediately; add `flush_interval -1` here if you ever proxy a
			# streaming response Caddy cannot classify.

			# -------------------------------------------------------------
			# Deploy-window buffering: hold the request instead of 502ing
			# while this container is being recreated.
			#
			# deploy/deploy.sh step 4/5 (`up -d --no-build`) stops and
			# recreates api and frontend, so for a few seconds `api:8080`
			# refuses connections and every request landing in that window
			# would be a hard 502. With a try duration set, Caddy re-dials
			# every lb_try_interval for up to that long and the client sees
			# a slow response instead of an error.
			#
			# SAFE FOR EVERY METHOD, POST/PUT/DELETE included, because these
			# retries only ever fire at DIAL time. Per the reverse_proxy
			# docs on lb_retry_match: "A request must match this condition
			# in order to be retried if the connection to the upstream
			# succeeded but the subsequent round-trip failed. If the
			# connection to the upstream failed, a retry is always allowed."
			# A refused dial means the api never received the request, so
			# replaying it cannot double-charge, double-upload or
			# double-delete anything. lb_retry_match is deliberately NOT set
			# here: its default already limits the *mid-request* case to GET
			# ("By default, only GET requests are retried"), which is the
			# conservative half of the behaviour and the half we want kept.
			#
			# 30s covers a whole recreate — stop (a drained api exits well
			# inside its 30s stop_grace_period), start, Go boot, /readyz
			# green. docker-compose.prod.yml budgets the api healthcheck the
			# same 30s start_period.
			#
			# lb_retries is left unset on purpose: it caps retries by COUNT,
			# and "retries may stop early if the duration is reached … the
			# retry duration takes precedence over the retry count", so a
			# count could only end the wait early. 250ms is the documented
			# default, written out because it is the knob that decides how
			# fast the returning api is picked up (never set it to 0 — the
			# docs warn a zero interval with a non-zero duration can spin
			# the CPU). Each attempt is itself bounded by the transport's
			# 3s default dial_timeout, but a refused connection fails
			# immediately, so the interval is what paces the loop.
			lb_try_duration 30s
			lb_try_interval 250ms
		}
	}

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	# 4. Everything else -> the Next.js frontend.
	#
	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated in the order written,
	# so this is the fallthrough. Compression is scoped HERE (HTML, JS, CSS,
	# JSON payloads of server actions) and nowhere else. zstd is offered first
	# for clients that support it.
	handle {
		encode zstd gzip
		reverse_proxy frontend:3000 {
			# Same deploy-window buffering as the api block above (see there
			# for why dial-time retries are safe for every method), with a
			# larger budget: the frontend is NOT recreated in parallel with
			# the api, it waits for it. docker-compose.yml declares
			# `depends_on: api: condition: service_healthy`, so its outage is
			# the api's whole recreate PLUS its own boot. The api healthcheck
			# is allowed a 30s start_period and the frontend's own is 20s, so
			# 45s is a realistic ceiling for the pair without holding a
			# browser tab open absurdly long.
			lb_try_duration 45s
			lb_try_interval 250ms
		}
	}
}

# Optional: send the bare www host to the canonical origin. Uncomment together
# with a www DNS record — Caddy will provision a certificate for it.
# www.example.com {
# 	redir https://example.com{uri} permanent
# }

# ============================================================================
# ALTERNATIVE (NOT RECOMMENDED): split subdomains
# ============================================================================
# Kept only so the tradeoff is visible. If you use this layout, PUBLIC_BASE_URL
# can only name ONE origin, and whichever you choose is wrong for half the
# product:
#
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com
#     -> OAuth callbacks, NodeInfo and ActivityPub actor URIs are correct,
#        but every RSS <link>, every <loc> in /sitemap.xml and every oEmbed
#        iframe src points at api.example.com/videos/<id>, which the api does
#        not serve. They all 404. A sitemap full of 404s is worse for SEO than
#        no sitemap at all.
#
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://example.com
#     -> feeds/sitemap/oEmbed are correct, but the OAuth redirect_uri no longer
#        matches what the api serves and federation actor URIs become
#        unresolvable.
#
# Making this layout correct requires a Go change: a second config value
# (PUBLIC_WEB_BASE_URL, defaulting to PUBLIC_BASE_URL) returned by
# distribution.go's webOrigin(), while thumbnailURL() stays on PUBLIC_BASE_URL
# because that path really is an API route. See plan step 9.
#
# api.example.com {
# 	handle /metrics {
# 		respond 404
# 	}
# 	reverse_proxy api:8080
# }
#
# example.com {
# 	encode zstd gzip
# 	reverse_proxy frontend:3000
# }
